### How do you define and use an enum?

```java
public enum AccountType {
    SAVINGS, CURRENT, LOAN
}

public class Account {
    private AccountType type;

    public Account(AccountType type) {
        this.type = type;  // can only be SAVINGS, CURRENT, or LOAN
    }
}

Account a = new Account(AccountType.SAVINGS);
// Account b = new Account(99);  // COMPILE ERROR: type mismatch
```

### Why not use int constants?

```java
// BAD: int constants
public static final int SAVINGS = 1;
public static final int CURRENT = 2;
public static final int LOAN = 3;

public void process(int accountType) {
    // Nothing stops: process(99) or process(-1)
}

// GOOD: enum
public void process(AccountType type) {
    // Only SAVINGS, CURRENT, LOAN are possible
}
```

### How do you add fields and methods to an enum?

```java
public enum AccountType {
    SAVINGS("Savings Account", 4.0),
    CURRENT("Current Account", 0.0),
    LOAN("Loan Account", 8.5);

    private final String displayName;
    private final double defaultRate;

    // Constructor: called once per constant at class load time
    AccountType(String displayName, double defaultRate) {
        this.displayName = displayName;
        this.defaultRate = defaultRate;
    }

    public String getDisplayName() { return displayName; }
    public double getDefaultRate() { return defaultRate; }
}

System.out.println(AccountType.SAVINGS.getDisplayName());  // "Savings Account"
System.out.println(AccountType.LOAN.getDefaultRate());      // 8.5
```

### How do you use enum in switch?

```java
AccountType type = AccountType.SAVINGS;

// Classic switch
switch (type) {
    case SAVINGS: System.out.println("4% interest"); break;
    case CURRENT: System.out.println("No interest"); break;
    case LOAN:    System.out.println("8.5% EMI"); break;
}

// Switch expression (Java 14+)
String desc = switch (type) {
    case SAVINGS -> "4% interest";
    case CURRENT -> "No interest";
    case LOAN    -> "8.5% EMI";
};
```

### How do you compare enums?

```java
AccountType a = AccountType.SAVINGS;
AccountType b = AccountType.SAVINGS;

// == is correct and preferred for enums
System.out.println(a == b);       // true (singletons)
System.out.println(a.equals(b));  // true (also works)

// == is null-safe
AccountType c = null;
System.out.println(c == AccountType.SAVINGS);  // false (no NPE)
// c.equals(AccountType.SAVINGS)               // NPE!
```

### What are EnumSet and EnumMap?

```java
// EnumSet: backed by a bitmask – extremely fast
EnumSet<AccountType> interestBearing = EnumSet.of(AccountType.SAVINGS, AccountType.LOAN);
System.out.println(interestBearing.contains(AccountType.CURRENT));  // false

// EnumMap: backed by an array indexed by ordinal – fast and memory-efficient
EnumMap<AccountType, BigDecimal> rates = new EnumMap<>(AccountType.class);
rates.put(AccountType.SAVINGS, new BigDecimal("4.0"));
rates.put(AccountType.LOAN, new BigDecimal("8.5"));
```

### What are common mistakes?

- **Using String constants instead of enum.** `"SAVINGS"` can be misspelled. `AccountType.SAVINGS` cannot.
- **Using .equals() instead of ==.** Both work, but == is null-safe and faster.
- **Forgetting that enum constructors are private.** You cannot write `new AccountType(...)` outside the enum.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Enums implement Serializable.** They survive serialization correctly – the JVM returns the existing singleton.
- **Enums can implement interfaces.** `enum AccountType implements Displayable { ... }`.
- **Abstract methods on enums** force each constant to provide an implementation: each constant becomes an anonymous class.
- **JPA @Enumerated** maps enums to database columns: `@Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)` stores the name, `EnumType.ORDINAL` stores the position (fragile, avoid).
